 There's a famous quote that floats around which is attributed to British

2
00:00:04,280 --> 00:00:08,680
 mathematician George Box and it goes something like all models are wrong but

3
00:00:08,680 --> 00:00:13,360
 some models are useful and as cliche as that quotes become I've kind of

4
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:16,720
 included it now because it's something that's really important for you to

5
00:00:16,720 --> 00:00:21,480
 understand. Every model that you build is just that a model and a

6
00:00:21,480 --> 00:00:25,160
 approximation to reality and it's only as good as the assumptions you've made

7
00:00:25,160 --> 00:00:28,120
 when you're building the model and the confidence that you have in the

8
00:00:28,120 --> 00:00:32,000
 inputs that you're feeding it. Remember we build models to help us make

9
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:36,680
 decisions that would otherwise be too costly or too time intensive for us to

10
00:00:36,680 --> 00:00:42,080
 guess and check but obviously with infinite time and infinite money the best

11
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:46,120
 case scenario is to test the components you plan to use in the situation you

12
00:00:46,120 --> 00:00:51,880
 plan to use them. This isn't always or even usually possible and as a result

13
00:00:51,880 --> 00:00:55,920
 we're here as engineers constructing these models these approximations to

14
00:00:55,920 --> 00:01:00,000
 reality to help us make some of these decisions. Our job is to build these

15
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,080
 models close enough to reality that we can draw some meaningful conclusions

16
00:01:04,080 --> 00:01:07,520
 from them. In order for us to have this confidence we need to perform a process

17
00:01:07,520 --> 00:01:11,160
 known as model validation. This usually entails feeding our model and

18
00:01:11,160 --> 00:01:16,000
 known input and comparing the output the predictions to either experimental

19
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:20,400
 measurements or sometimes theoretical predictions based on the physics of the

20
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:24,600
 problem. Sometimes you can't physically validate your model for the exact set of

21
00:01:24,680 --> 00:01:28,080
 conditions or the exact group of components that you're interested in. When

22
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:31,120
 something like this happens you need to validate and unit test as many

23
00:01:31,120 --> 00:01:34,760
 components as you can in as many environments as you can. To give an

24
00:01:34,760 --> 00:01:38,480
 example for the Mars Rover they unit tested all of the components and then

25
00:01:38,480 --> 00:01:41,760
 did full system testing conditions that they believe would be similar to Mars

