00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:02,000
 in the aerodynamics package for our car. It was the first

32
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:06,080
 area kit that we designed for a car here at Newcastle. In this package the two

33
00:02:06,080 --> 00:02:09,640
 wings in the under-trailer design using a technique called computational fluid

34
00:02:09,640 --> 00:02:14,560
 dynamics CFD. This in itself is a model which simulates the airflow over a

35
00:02:14,560 --> 00:02:20,640
 body. Now CFD is notoriously sensitive to your inputs and to the quality of the

36
00:02:20,640 --> 00:02:24,320
 model that you're feeding it and so validation is crucially important to make

37
00:02:24,320 --> 00:02:29,000
 sure the results you're getting out of it aren't garbage. Now we don't have a

38
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:32,920
 wind tunnel that can fit a car here at Newcastle. In fact I think the nearest

39
00:02:32,920 --> 00:02:36,720
 one that was suitable would be down in Melbourne in Monash and we couldn't get

40
00:02:36,720 --> 00:02:40,800
 access to it. So to perform our validation we did a combination of small-scale

41
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:45,440
 wind tunnel testing and we also performed some large-scale drag testing by

42
00:02:45,440 --> 00:02:49,680
 using an analog. We made a bit of plywood that we calculated would provide about as

43
00:02:49,680 --> 00:02:53,440
 much drag as our CFD predicted the error kit would make. We

44
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:56,880
 extracted to the car and we did some testing and we measured that drag using

45
00:02:56,880 --> 00:03:00,040
 the information we had available. I think in that case we used other fuel

46
00:03:00,040 --> 00:03:03,560
 level and the amount of fuel consumption. And through that testing we were able

47
00:03:03,560 --> 00:03:07,080
 to validate the results we were getting out of CFD were in the realm of reality.

48
00:03:07,080 --> 00:03:11,080
 In our case it was a bit of a contrived example. No one was going to die if we

49
00:03:11,080 --> 00:03:14,240
 didn't validate the model that our race car wings were making as much downforce

50
00:03:14,240 --> 00:03:17,920
 and drag as we were expecting. But this is crucially important in the real

51
00:03:17,920 --> 00:03:22,200
 world. Your models have the potential to kill people and it's really really

52
00:03:22,200 --> 00:03:25,120
 important that you validate that the predictions that you're making are correct.

53
00:03:25,120 --> 00:03:29,680
 Just like me and the SAE team in 2016 you won't always have the ability to

54
00:03:29,680 --> 00:03:33,280
 conduct full one-to-one-scale validation in the circumstances that are

55
00:03:33,280 --> 00:03:37,000
 perfectly representative of the situation that your predictions are

56
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:41,560
 going to be used in. But the ethical responsibility is on you the modeler to

57
00:03:41,560 --> 00:03:45,120
 make sure that people are using your model correctly. Part of this is about

58
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:48,360
 disclosing the assumptions you've made clearly at the top of your model. And

59
00:03:48,360 --> 00:03:51,360
 this is a process that we're going to talk about in this course when we start

60
00:03:51,360 --> 00:03:55,000
 building mathematical models. And part of it is making sure that the model is

